{
  "image_name": "a02074fa57d335f2acd55113fc2efea2.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes the “lifecycle” of plastic debris in coastal and open-ocean waters, showing how plastic enters, moves through, and is transformed within the marine environment. Plastic comes from both land and aquatic sources, including coastal waste, wastewater treatment plants, and lost fishing gear. Some debris remains at the surface as floating plastic, especially lighter polymers, while much of it is carried downward into deeper water.\n\nA key message is that only a small fraction is visible at the surface, while most of the plastic is “missing” below. In the sunlit photic zone, particles can be colonized by microbes and other organisms. This biological growth, along with degradation or fouling, appears to increase sinking, sending denser or altered plastics toward the deep chlorophyll maximum, the twilight zone, and eventually the ocean floor.\n\nThe diagram also emphasizes ecological interactions. Filter feeders and other marine organisms may encounter or ingest plastic, and fish are shown consuming particles in deeper layers. Fecal pellets may help transport plastic downward as well. Overall, the image presents plastic debris as dynamic material that is redistributed vertically by buoyancy, biology, and food-web processes rather than simply accumulating at the sea surface.",
